  "query_preview": "..\nA weak upper-level low is producing a few, short-lived isolated\nshowers to the southwest of Guam's coastal water. Overall, a dry \npattern regime of isolated showers are expected through the middle of\nnext week. \n\nMarine...\nCombined seas are 3 to 4 feet over the region. These seas are\nexpected to fall to 2 to 3 feet over the next few days. Winds are \nexpected to be light to gentle. Overall benign seas are expected\nthrough the weekend.\n\nEastern Micronesia...\nIn the short term (the next 72 hours), pretty tame conditions will\ncontinue with just isolated showers with light to gentle winds across\nall of Eastern Micronesia. Attention then turns to a fairly strong\ntropical wave set to cross the Date Line tomorrow morning or\nafternoon. This wave has a healthy amount of convection with it,\nwhich will slowly spread over the region from east to west over the \nweekend and into next week. The increasing rainfall potential looks \nto reach Majuro Thursday night (tomorrow night), and Kosrae over the \nweekend. Pohnpei may have to wait until we get towards the middle of \nnext week. Some of these showers, if model trends continue, may be \nlocally heavy at times and bring some gusty winds. Stay Tuned!\n\nFor our mariners, light to gentle winds will prevail through the\nweekend and for the first half of next week. However, rainfall\npotential will increase from east to west with time, as mentioned\nabove. Some showers could bring gusty winds, with Majuro and portions\nof the RMI to first see this potential towards the end of the week.\nSeas of 3 to 5 feet may build a foot next week.\n\nWestern Micronesia...\nSatellite imagery shows isolated showers across the region. \nAltimetry shows combined seas between 2 and 5 feet.\n\nThe forecast continues to remain near status quo with only minor \nadjustments. Summer doldrums look to continue through the rest of the \nweek across western Micronesia. The current dry trade-wind regime \nwill generally favor isolated to low-end (30%) scattered showers \nover the coming days, except when stronger troughs move across the \nregion. A surface trough seen across western Yap State near Sorol \nand Fais will bring a slight increase in PoPs (40%) to Yap on \nThursday and Palau Thursday night. Meanwhile, low level convergence \ncontinues across Weno, which should assist with some scattered \nshowers and isolated thunderstorms after midnight tonight. In \nbetween these more evident features, weak trade-wind showers are \nexpected. \n\nNo change was made to the marine forecast. Combined seas remain \nbetween 2 and 5 feet and will continue to be benign through the \nforecast. Seas may fall another foot or two for Palau and Yap later \nthis week, leading to a light swell.",
